\chapter{Exotract discovery and native archives}
\label{ch:94}
\index{Exotract discovery and native archives}

Exotract's catalog and discovery frontend select source headers and extract typed declaration data for a target. A supported build recipe can produce a C archive from one planned implementation translation unit and its documented companions. Some profiles provide declaration inspection only. The locked workflow identifies inputs, toolchain and archive output. The C archive is a provider that a native consumer may link.

\section{Distinguish two linkage cases}
Compile a strict separate C consumer against a supported archive and check that symbol resolution succeeds without activating implementation macros in the caller. For a selected static-inline function, inspect the documented \OnxCodeTerm{wrap-inline} route and signature constraints before requesting a raw forwarding artifact. An archive can contain valid kernel code and still lack an externally visible symbol for an inline function. Passing an accepted archive to the On1x build helper changes how the implementation is supplied. It does not establish source safety or ownership admission for its API.

\section{Trace a forwarder into a separate client}
In the media timebase fixture the C caller includes
\OnxCodeTerm{on1x-inline.h}. Look for the generated prefix
\OnxCodeTerm{on1x\_inline\_v1\_} on the call to rescale. That name denotes
a new external function, not a change of linkage on the original
definition. The generated implementation can call the original because
it includes the kernel header in its own translation unit. A client
object built from the generated declaration can therefore be linked
against the archive without recompiling the client's view of rescale
as a global function.

Follow one call across this seam. The source unit is $1001/30000$
seconds per tick and the destination is $1/48000$. At 30000 source ticks
the elapsed time is 1001 seconds, hence the client expects 48048000
destination ticks. The integer travels back through a caller-supplied
writable slot; the function result reports success. With one negative
source tick the exact value is fractional and truncation toward zero
gives $-1601$. This case would distinguish a forwarder that rounded
down from one that actually preserved the kernel behavior.

Set the output slot to a known value before requesting an invalid
denominator. Check both the false result and the unchanged slot. This
exercise tests a property of that particular function, not a promise
made by all forwarding wrappers. The producer checks the selected
prototype and record layout; it does not allocate output storage for a
caller or certify how long an address remains writable.

Inspect the symbol map when debugging this client: the external
reference to resolve is the prefixed forwarding name, not the
unprefixed internal definition reported by declaration discovery.

